::: {.doc-subject}

Count all trails (walks with distinct edges) from source to target.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L68233-L68361){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.count_trails(
    source,
    target,
    max_trails=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Count trails](../assets/graphs/count-trails.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A directed path A, B, C, D with a loop B, E, F back to B. Two trails reach D from A: the plain path in the accent color, and a teal one that goes around the loop and passes B twice without reusing an edge. The card lists both and the count, 2." width="100%"}

A *trail* is a walk where all edges are distinct, but nodes may repeat. This is different
from:

- *path*: no repeated nodes or edges (most restrictive)
- *trail*: no repeated edges, but nodes may repeat
- *walk*: nodes and edges may repeat freely (least restrictive)

Because a trail may loop through a node more than once, there are usually more trails
than simple paths between two nodes. Counting them is useful when you care about how many
distinct ways something can travel through a network without reusing a link, for example
to gauge routing redundancy or to compare how "tangled" two parts of a network are.

Each trail ends the first time it reaches `target=`; trails that pass through the target
and come back to it later are not counted separately. On a directed graph edges are only
followed in their own direction; on an undirected graph each edge can be crossed in either
direction, but only once. Parallel edges count as distinct edges.

Uses iterative depth-first search with edge tracking. The count can grow exponentially on
dense graphs, so use the `max_trails=` parameter to limit computation. Use
`enumerate_trails()` to get the trails themselves. The method does not modify the graph.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The node the trails start from, given as an integer node ID or a string label.

<code>[target]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The node the trails end at, given as an integer node ID or a string label. If it is the
    same node as `source=`, the result is `1` (the trivial empty trail).

<code>[max_trails]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Upper bound on the counting work. If `None` (default), every trail is counted, however
    long that takes. Otherwise the search stops as soon as `max_trails=` trails have been
    found and the method returns `None`, so a numeric result is only returned when the
    true count is *less than* `max_trails=`.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of distinct trails from source to target. Returns `None` if the count reached
    `max_trails=` (the true count is at least that large). Returns `1` if source and
    target are the same node.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If source or target node not found in graph.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

A trail generalizes a path: it forbids reusing edges but allows revisiting nodes,
which places it strictly between a walk (nothing forbidden) and a path (nothing
repeated). Under the hood this is an iterative depth-first search with an explicit
stack, tracking a visited-edge set that gets popped on backtrack. The count grows
fast: between two nodes of a complete graph there are $2$ trails in $K_3$, $7$ in
$K_4$, $58$ in $K_5$, and nearly $3{,}000$ in $K_6$, and moderately dense real
graphs run into the millions. Setting `max_trails=` short-circuits the search once
the cap is reached and returns `None`, which is essentially always the right choice
on real-world graphs. The trivial case `source == target` counts as one (empty)
trail. On undirected graphs, each edge can still be crossed in either direction, but
only once.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~enumerate_trails`) 

:   The trails themselves.

[](`~all_simple_paths`) 

:   Paths that never revisit a node.

[](`~count_walks`) 

:   Count the walks of a given length between every pair of nodes.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

In a triangle there are two trails from `A` to `C`, straight there or through `B`:

::: {#ccb721b3 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "A")])

graph.count_trails("A", "C")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
2
```
:::
:::


### Crossing the bridges

`konigsberg()` is the city of Königsberg as Euler studied it in 1736, with seven bridges
joining its two islands and two river banks. A walk from the North Bank to the South Bank
that never crosses a bridge twice can return to an island it has already been on, so there
are more of these trails than simple paths:

::: {#668f5a12 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
konigsberg = ec.konigsberg()

(
    konigsberg.count_trails("North Bank", "South Bank"),
    len(konigsberg.all_simple_paths("North Bank", "South Bank")),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(23, 9)
```
:::
:::


::: {#c89a9d48 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
konigsberg.plot(
    layout="pos", color_by="kind", label_by="label",
    edge_label_by="bridge",
)
```

